From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 00:18:18 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] secret_vault, settings_ui: a secret declares the variable a
 jail sees and the hosts it may reach
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Proposal §6.53, step 1 of 5 (operator, 2026-11-27): the vault learns what a
jail needs to use a secret without ever seeing it. `0162` stored a value
under azukari's `service`/`account` and nothing else, and could only store,
list and delete -- no way for Zeo to read a value back to hand to ai-jail.

secret_vault:
- JailUse { env_name, hosts }: the variable a jailed agent sees (it will hold
  a per-session placeholder, never the value) and the hosts ai-jail's egress
  proxy may send the real value to. A variable is letters, digits and `_`,
  not first a digit. A host is a DNS name with a dot -- no scheme, port,
  path, wildcard or IP address -- because the proxy mints a certificate for
  exactly that name and ai-jail binds the secret to it; lowercased, trailing
  dot dropped, sorted, deduplicated, one to MAX_HOSTS (8).
- The declaration lives on the keyring item itself, as `zeo-env` and
  `zeo-hosts`, not in settings.json: deleting the secret deletes its
  declaration too, and nothing in settings can point a variable at a value
  the keyring does not hold. Settings will only choose, by variable name,
  which jails receive one (the Capabilities of `0152`; a later step). An item
  whose declaration does not read back -- edited outside Zeo -- declares
  none, so no jail receives it.
- store() writes the new item first and deletes the old ones after, so a
  failed save keeps the previous secret; a save that changes the hosts no
  longer leaves two items azukari would refuse as ambiguous.
- read(): the value in memory zeroized on drop (zeroize, already a
  workspace dependency). Refused when no item, or more than one, answers the
  reference: handing a jail the wrong credential is worse than none.

settings_ui, the Secrets page:
- two optional fields under the value, "variable a jail sees" and "hosts it
  may go to" (comma- or space-separated); both empty is a secret for azukari
  alone, one without the other is refused with the reason;
- a row shows `NAME → host, host` beside the reference, never the value;
- the store log line names the variable and the number of hosts.
- four new strings in all nine locales; the seven not reviewed by a native
  speaker keep their files' existing "not reviewed" header.

Nothing reaches a jail yet: ai-jail's `--secret` swaps only in plain HTTP
(CONNECT stays opaque), so wiring this before the fork's HTTPS injection
would hand a placeholder nobody replaces.

Tests: secret_vault_tests (a jail use normalizes its hosts; refuses a
variable or host the proxy could not bind -- scheme, port, path, wildcard,
IP, localhost, more than eight; parses the page's two fields; the item's
attributes carry it and read back, and an edited one declares nothing) and
secrets_page_tests (the jail fields are optional but go together; a row
shows the jail use and never the value).

Against a real Secret Service, tests/live_keyring.rs (ignored unless
ZEO_VAULT_LIVE=1) now also saves one secret with one host, then with two:
one item remains, it lists the two hosts, and read() returns the last value.
